(202, 23, NULL, 'assistant', 'Abraham Lincoln, the 16th President of the United States, is one of the most significant figures in American history, known for leading the country through the Civil War and playing a crucial role in the abolition of slavery. He served as president from 1861 until his assassination in 1865.\n\n**Early Life and Career**\nBorn on February 12, 1809, in a one-room log cabin in Kentucky, Lincoln grew up on the American frontier. His family moved to Indiana when he was seven and later to Illinois. Lincoln\'s formal education was very limited, amounting to roughly a year, but he was largely self-educated, reading voraciously and learning from life experiences.\n\nHe held various jobs, including boatman, store clerk, and surveyor. Lincoln’s political career began in Illinois, where he was admitted to the bar in 1836 and practiced law. He served in the Illinois state legislature for eight years and was elected to the U.S. House of Representatives in 1846. In 1842, he married Mary Todd, and they had four sons, though only one lived to adulthood.\n\n**Rise to National Prominence and Presidency**\nLincoln became a leader in the new Republican Party, largely due to his strong opposition to the expansion of slavery into new territories, a stance solidified after the Kansas-Nebraska Act of 1854. His debates with Stephen A. Douglas during the 1858 Senate campaign brought him national recognition.\n\nIn 1860, Lincoln won the presidential election, becoming the first Republican president. His victory, however, prompted a majority of the slave states to secede from the Union and form the Confederate States of America, leading to the outbreak of the Civil War.\n\n**The Civil War and Emancipation**\nLincoln\'s presidency was dominated by the Civil War, which began weeks after his inauguration with the Confederate attack on Fort Sumter. He was resolute in his commitment to preserving the Union, even if it meant war. As commander-in-chief, he navigated both the political and military challenges of the war, making difficult decisions such as suspending habeas corpus in Maryland to suppress Confederate sympathizers and cycling through various military commanders before settling on Ulysses S. Grant.\n\nA pivotal moment in his presidency was the issuance of the Emancipation Proclamation on January 1, 1863. This executive order declared that all enslaved people in Confederate territory were to be free, transforming the character of the war into a fight for a \"new birth of freedom\" and the abolition of slavery. In November 1863, Lincoln delivered his famous Gettysburg Address, further emphasizing the ideals of liberty and equality.\n\nLincoln was re-elected in 1864, and he was instrumental in the passage of the Thirteenth Amendment, which officially abolished slavery in the United States.\n\n**Assassination**\nJust days after the Civil War effectively ended with the surrender of Confederate General Robert E. Lee in April 1865, Abraham Lincoln was assassinated. On the evening of April 14, 1865, while attending a performance of \"Our American Cousin\" at Ford\'s Theatre in Washington, D.C., he was shot in the head by John Wilkes Booth, a Confederate sympathizer and famous actor. Lincoln died the following morning, April 15, 1865, at the age of 56. His death plunged the nation into mourning and triggered one of the largest manhunts in American history.', '[{\"source_id\":null,\"source_name\":\"wikipedia.org\",\"type\":\"web\",\"source_url\":\"https:\\/\\/vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com\\/grounding-api-redirect\\/AUZIYQFVI2SG7YMjmFupa4BHuuhmq8JgZRhmiWcz1Go2koRKIUVKrXgTYiiwNv5cmk8P8Acf78_3MRaonA8oZ1g2fYIhNWFL81kG1S9PfHj4SrYcxBM0VNwUAc0th2yt8e6zwsBVED3yVdVQCg==\"},{\"source_id\":null,\"source_name\":\"wikipedia.org\",\"type\":\"web\",\"source_url\":\"https:\\/\\/vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com\\/grounding-api-redirect\\/AUZIYQHzHmvjHcEDYWjx_tFNQYbPZ6LV6NjWrIfmJs-Fi5VN2ZLPD9i8nrY7ub2_Fdj-AWZcZO7GPag8nCI3d8OdLwle0I9nbAtYztYrmVSi5s-